CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE harness_app.archive_old_orders(p_date IN DATE)
IS
  TYPE t_rows IS TABLE OF harness_app.orders%ROWTYPE;
  l_rows t_rows;

  CURSOR c_orders IS
    SELECT *
    FROM harness_app.orders
    WHERE order_date < p_date
    FOR UPDATE;
BEGIN
  OPEN c_orders;

  LOOP
    FETCH c_orders BULK COLLECT INTO l_rows LIMIT 10000;

    EXIT WHEN l_rows.COUNT = 0;

    FORALL i IN 1 .. l_rows.COUNT
      INSERT INTO harness_app.orders_archive VALUES l_rows(i);

    FORALL i IN 1 .. l_rows.COUNT
      DELETE FROM harness_app.orders
      WHERE order_id = l_rows(i).order_id;
  END LOOP;

  CLOSE c_orders;
END archive_old_orders;
/
```

Assumptions:

- “Older than a given date” is implemented as `order_date < p_date`. If you want the cutoff date to be included, change it to `order_date <= p_date`.
- If `p_date` is `NULL`, the predicate matches no rows and the procedure does nothing.
- The procedure does not commit or rollback; the caller controls the transaction.
- The cursor uses `FOR UPDATE` so that the rows fetched into the batch are locked before they are inserted into `ORDERS_ARCHIVE` and deleted from `ORDERS`.
- The batch order is not specified because the cursor has no `ORDER BY`. If deterministic batch order is required, add `ORDER BY order_id` to the cursor query.

